|~ `::construct`, the step that puts a value under a tag through the
   tag's constructor, `::T[…]`, and what is declared under it: its
   constructor and the refusals of that step. ~|

:construct
  |~~ The step that puts a payload under a tag, `::T[…]`, `::T{…}`,
      `::T(…)` or `::T"…"`, through the tag's constructor, which checks or
      builds the value. As a record, `:tag`
      is the tag and `:payload` the step of its payload. ::link~(::tag | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang/pipeline | doc)
  ~~|

:construct/holdsTheTagAndThePayload
  |~~ The step holds its tag and its payload. ~~|
  ::law~(~(::Box[1 2]) | first | [/tag /payload] | eq [::Box [1 2]])

:construct/construct
  |~~ The step of a constructor invocation from its record, `:tag` and
      `:payload`, which reads back as itself.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:construct/PayloadNotSchemaError
  |~~ A `::construct` is a map of `:tag`, a tag name, and `:payload`, a
      step, both given, and `:field` names the field that does not fit:
      the tag is written with its two colons, so the step `::Box[1]` is
      `{:tag ::Box :payload [1]} | tag ::construct`.
      ::link~(::construct | doc)
  ~~|

:construct/PayloadNotSchemaError/refusesATagThatIsAKeyword
  |~~ A `:tag` that is a keyword is refused, `:field` naming it. ~~|
  ::law~(::construct{:tag :Box :payload [1]}
    !| [type /field] | eq [::construct/PayloadNotSchemaError :tag])

:construct/PayloadNotSchemaError/refusesARecordWithoutAPayload
  |~~ A record without its payload is refused, `:field` naming it. ~~|
  ::law~({:tag ::Box} | tag ::construct
    !| [type /field] | eq [::construct/PayloadNotSchemaError :payload])

:construct/PayloadNotSchemaError/buildsTheStepOfATag
  |~~ A tag name and the step of a payload build the step. ~~|
  ::law~({:tag ::Box :payload [1]} | tag ::construct | [/] | tag ::quote | eq ~(::Box[1]))

:construct/ReadBackDiffersError
  |~~ The `::construct` prints as text that reads back as another step,
      and `:printed` is that text: a payload that opens with a name, a
      digit or a slash joins its tag, `::Box42` a name of its own. Such a
      payload is written as a group, the step `::Box(42)`, whose record
      holds the group, `{:tag ::Box :payload (~((42)) | first)}`.
      ::link~(::construct | doc)
  ~~|

:construct/ReadBackDiffersError/refusesAPayloadThatJoinsItsTag
  |~~ A number payload prints joined to its tag, which reads back as another name. ~~|
  ::law~(::construct{:tag ::Box :payload 42}
    !| [type /printed] | eq [::construct/ReadBackDiffersError "::Box42"])

:construct/ReadBackDiffersError/writesThePayloadAsAGroup
  |~~ A payload in a group reads back as itself. ~~|
  ::law~({:tag ::Box :payload (~((42)) | first)} | tag ::construct
    | [/] | tag ::quote | eq ~(::Box(42)))

:construct/TagNotKindError
  |~~ The tag before a payload addresses a kind, a label, a name declared
      by its page or a word, whose args it lays itself over,
      `::vec/take[2]`, or no declaration at all; a tag that addresses a
      name holding a value of its own names nothing to build by, and
      `:tag` is that tag. The value is read by its name, `limit`, and is
      put under a tag of its own, `::Limit(limit)`.
      ::link~(::construct | doc)
  ~~|

:construct/TagNotKindError/tag
  |~~ The tag, whose node is neither a label nor a word. ~~|
  ::tag

:construct/TagNotKindError/refusesTheTagOfAValue
  |~~ The tag of a name that holds a value builds nothing. ~~|
  ::law~(:limit 5 | ::limit[1]
    !| [type /tag /actualType] | eq [::construct/TagNotKindError ::limit ::number])

:construct/TagNotKindError/laysAWordsTagOverItsArgs
  |~~ The tag of a word lays itself over its args. ~~|
  ::law~(::vec/take[2] | type | eq ::vec/take)

:construct/TagNotKindError/putsTheValueUnderATagOfItsOwn
  |~~ The value of a name is put under a tag of its own. ~~|
  ::law~(:limit 5 | ::Limit(limit) | payload | eq 5)
